---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import EmailCapture from '../../components/EmailCapture.astro';
import { url } from '../../utils/url';
import { planFor, singleChangeFraction } from '../../utils/water-change-calc';

const nutrientExample = { current: 40, target: 10, gallons: 75 };
const dilutionExamples = [10, 20, 30].map((pct) => planFor(nutrientExample.current, nutrientExample.target, pct, nutrientExample.gallons)!);

const methods = [
  { name: 'DIY vodka', control: 'Precise, manual', cost: 'Cheapest', notes: 'Ethanol only — potent, least forgiving if you overshoot a dose.' },
  { name: 'DIY vinegar', control: 'Measured liquid dose', cost: 'Low', notes: 'Acetic acid; concentration differs from vodka. Do not swap liquids at the same milliliter dose.' },
  { name: 'Bottled liquid carbon (e.g. NO3:PO4-X)', control: 'Product-specific instructions', cost: 'Ongoing purchase', notes: 'Use the label for that product and your measured water volume; no universal carbon dose applies.' },
  { name: 'Biopellet reactor', control: 'Media quantity and reactor flow', cost: 'Reactor plus media', notes: 'A different delivery method that still needs monitoring and biomass export.' },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is NOPX (NOPOX) dosing?',
    a: "Red Sea's NO3:PO4-X is a bottled carbon source used for nutrient management. It feeds bacteria; the resulting biomass must be exported. Follow that product's instructions rather than treating it as the same concentration as vodka or vinegar.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Is carbon dosing the same as the Alk/Ca/Mg dosing my calculator covers?',
    a: "No — they're unrelated. Alk/Ca/Mg dosing (two-part or kalkwasser) replaces what corals consume building skeleton. Carbon dosing feeds bacteria to strip nitrate and phosphate — a nutrient-export method with nothing to do with calcification chemistry. Plenty of reefers run both at once for different reasons.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need a protein skimmer to carbon dose?',
    a: "Yes, effectively. Carbon dosing works by growing bacterial biomass that has to be removed from the system — a skimmer is how you export it. Without strong, healthy skimming, that biomass and the oxygen it consumes just build up in the tank instead of leaving it.",
  },
  {
    q: 'How fast should I raise my carbon dose?',
    a: "Use the selected product's instructions and actual water volume; vodka, vinegar, and bottled carbon do not share one milliliter dose. Record nitrate and phosphate before adjusting. Stop dosing and add aeration if water turns cloudy or fish breathe heavily. The alkalinity, calcium, and magnesium calculator does not calculate carbon doses.",
  },
];

const faqLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

const headline = "Carbon dosing for reef tanks: nutrient export & risks";
---
<Base
  headline={headline}
  title="Carbon Dosing for Reef Tanks: Vodka & Vinegar"
  description="Learn how carbon dosing lowers reef nitrate and phosphate. Compare vodka, vinegar, bottled carbon, and biopellets, with ramp-up and oxygen warnings."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/guides' }, { name: 'Carbon Dosing', href: '/guides/carbon-dosing' }]}
>
  <section class="container prose" style="padding-top:40px;">
    <p class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--brand-2)">Guide · Nutrient export</p>
    <h1>{headline}</h1>
    <p class="lede-dark">Carbon dosing adds an organic carbon source to support bacteria that consume nitrate and phosphate. The nutrients leave when you export that biomass, usually by skimming. It is a nutrient-management method, not a calcium or alkalinity supplement, and excess bacterial growth can lower oxygen. Measure nutrients, check export, and use instructions for the chosen liquid before starting.</p>

    <Disclosure />

    <h2>The one-line answer</h2>
    <p>Carbon dosing means adding an organic carbon source — vodka, vinegar, or a bottled product like NOPX — to feed bacteria that consume nitrate and phosphate as they grow. Skim off the resulting bacterial biomass (or crop it off biopellets in a reactor) and the nutrients leave with it. Start low, raise slowly, and keep your skimmer strong — the bacteria you're growing also consume oxygen, and that's the real risk here.</p>

    <h2>How it actually works</h2>
    <p>Bacterial growth depends on available carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus, and other conditions. Added carbon may support nutrient uptake, but it does not prove that carbon shortage caused your tank's nitrate or phosphate level. Nutrient levels and response vary by tank. The resulting biomass must be exported; measure both nitrate and phosphate and follow the chosen product's instructions rather than calculating a universal dose from an ocean nutrient ratio.</p>

    <h2>Not to be confused with Alk/Ca/Mg dosing</h2>
    <p>This is a completely different topic from what our <a href={url('/tools/reef-dosing-calculator')}>dosing calculator</a> or <a href={url('/guides/kalkwasser-dosing')}>kalkwasser guide</a> cover. Those replace what corals consume building calcium carbonate skeleton. Carbon dosing feeds bacteria to strip excess nutrients — nothing to do with calcification chemistry. Plenty of reefers run both a calcification dosing routine and a carbon dosing routine at the same time, for entirely unrelated reasons.</p>

    <h2>Four ways to supply carbon</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison table, scroll for all columns">
      <table class="specs">
        <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Control</th><th>Cost</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {methods.map((m) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{m.name}</strong></td><td>{m.control}</td><td>{m.cost}</td><td style="color:var(--muted)">{m.notes}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="rnote">Liquid strengths and instructions differ. Do not transfer a milliliter schedule between products. If you later automate an established routine, compare <a href={url('/reviews/best-reef-dosing-pumps')}>reef dosing pumps</a> by calibration, small-dose capability, and controller needs.</p>

    <h2>What water changes can remove before adding carbon</h2>
    <p>Use a dilution baseline to compare approaches. For {nutrientExample.gallons} gallons at {nutrientExample.current} ppm nitrate, the repo's water-change model gives the following paths to {nutrientExample.target} ppm or less. The model adds no new nitrate between changes and assumes replacement water has none.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison table, scroll for all columns"><table class="specs"><thead><tr><th>Each change</th><th>Changes needed</th><th>Total new water</th><th>Modeled final nitrate</th></tr></thead><tbody>{dilutionExamples.map((p) => <tr><td>{p.pct}%</td><td>{p.changes}</td><td>{p.totalNewWaterGal.toFixed(1)} gal</td><td>{p.levels[p.levels.length - 1].toFixed(1)} ppm</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>A single change would require {(singleChangeFraction(nutrientExample.current, nutrientExample.target) * 100).toFixed(0)}% in this math. That is a comparison, not a recommendation for a sudden large change. Feeding and waste add nutrients in real tanks. Use the <a href={url('/tools/water-change-calculator')}>water change calculator</a> for your own readings, and the <a href={url('/tools/protein-skimmer-calculator')}>skimmer sizing tool</a> to check export equipment.</p>

    <h2>The ramp-up protocol</h2>
    <p>First record actual water volume, nitrate, phosphate, the chosen product and its concentration, and the label's starting instructions. Keep a record of doses and readings before adjusting. There is no single safe milliliter schedule for vodka, vinegar, and every bottled blend. Keep export and aeration working, and stop if warning signs appear. Do not use the mineral-dosing calculator to invent a carbon dose.</p>

    <h2>The real risk: bacterial bloom and oxygen crash</h2>
    <p>The bacteria you're feeding consume dissolved oxygen as they multiply — dose too much, too fast, and you can trigger a bloom that outpaces your skimmer's ability to export it, crashing oxygen levels in the process. Warning signs: cloudy water, fish breathing heavily at the surface, or a skimmer cup filling far faster than usual. Treat any of those as a stop signal, not a sign to keep pushing. If it happens: stop dosing immediately, add surface agitation or extra aeration, and do a partial water change. This is also why carbon dosing without a skimmer isn't really an option — the entire method depends on exporting the biomass you're growing, and a skimmer is how that happens.</p>

    <h2>Which method fits your tank?</h2>
    <p><strong>Considering a DIY liquid:</strong> choose a documented method and match its concentration exactly. A household label alone does not make a liquid suitable for a dosing schedule written for something else.</p>
    <p><strong>Want product-specific instructions:</strong> compare a bottled carbon source and its ongoing cost with a documented DIY method. Follow the selected method rather than combining their dose schedules.</p>
    <p><strong>Considering a reactor:</strong> plan space, flow, media maintenance, and regular nutrient checks. Biopellets do not remove the need to monitor the aquarium.</p>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    {faqs.map((f) => (
      <>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;">{f.q}</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--muted)">{f.a}</p>
      </>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <EmailCapture />
  </section>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqLd)} />
</Base>

<style>
  .lede-dark { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .tablewrap { overflow-x: auto; background: #fff; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); box-shadow: var(--shadow); margin: 18px 0; }
  table.specs { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .92rem; min-width: 640px; }
  table.specs th { text-align: left; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: .78rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .08em; color: var(--muted); padding: 12px 14px; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line); }
  table.specs td { padding: 10px 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); vertical-align: top; }
  table.specs tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }
</style>
